xnu 0365: never write a catalog fork with more bytes than blocks

The volume keeps gaining records that say a size and own too few blocks --
`knowledgeC.db-shm` at 32768 bytes with one block, and a fresh 37-byte file with
none every boot -- and each of those records is refused by `hfs_getnewvnode`
(`EINVAL`) and marks the volume on every lookup.

`ExtendFileC`'s deferred path (`kEFDeferMask`) grows `ff_size` and `ff_blocks`
together without touching the disk. When the reservation is converted it is
given back first (`ff_blocks -= loanedBlocks`, `ff_unallocblocks = 0`) and
re-allocated for real, so a failure there -- from `BlockAllocate`,
`CreateExtentRecord` or `UpdateExtentRecord` -- leaves the fork with the size
advanced and the blocks still missing. Nothing on that path trims the size again.

`hfs_prepare_fork_for_update` is the last gate before `cat_update` and its stated
job is to keep a fork from claiming data it cannot back, but its fast path only
looks at invalid ranges and deferred blocks:

    if (!ff->ff_unallocblocks && ff->ff_size <= max_size)
        return cf;              /* size 32768, blocks 1, written as is */

Make the fast path also require that the size fits the blocks the fork owns. A
fork that does not fit falls through to the existing trims, which cap the size at
`cf_blocks * block_size` and at the first invalid range, so the record that
reaches the catalog is always one whose size its blocks can hold. The file loses
the bytes that were never allocated; nothing else changes.
diff --git a/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/hfs_vnops.c b/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/hfs_vnops.c
index d210179..7ebbdd8 100644
--- a/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/hfs_vnops.c
+++ b/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/hfs_vnops.c
@@ -6210,7 +6210,18 @@ hfs_prepare_fork_for_update(filefork_t *ff,
 	if (!TAILQ_EMPTY(&ff->ff_invalidranges))
 		max_size = TAILQ_FIRST(&ff->ff_invalidranges)->rl_start;
 
-	if (!ff->ff_unallocblocks && ff->ff_size <= max_size)
+	/*
+	 * MONDRIAN: also refuse the fast path when the fork owns fewer blocks
+	 * than its size needs. `ExtendFileC` gives the deferred blocks back
+	 * before it allocates for real (`ff_blocks -= loanedBlocks`), and if
+	 * that allocation or its extent record fails the loop breaks with the
+	 * size already advanced -- measured as a 32768-byte file with one block.
+	 * Writing that record is what makes `hfs_getnewvnode` refuse the file
+	 * with EINVAL on every lookup. Trimming it here stores the bytes the
+	 * fork can actually back, and the record stays legal.
+	 */
+	if (!ff->ff_unallocblocks && ff->ff_size <= max_size &&
+	    ff->ff_size <= hfs_blk_to_bytes(ff->ff_blocks, block_size))
 		return cf; // Nothing to do
 
 	if (ff->ff_blocks < ff->ff_unallocblocks) {
